# -*- mode:org; coding:utf-8 -*-
#+TITLE: vl-catch-all-apply-optional-arglist — BricsCAD accepts (vl-catch-all-apply 'f) with no argument list, clautolisp requires two arguments
#+STATUS: DONE
#+PRIORITY: P1
#+TYPE: divergence
#+DEPENDS: -
#+BRANCH: fix-vl-catch-all-apply-optional-arglist

* Context

Found 2026-08-16 while verifying =macroman-encoding-name= end to end:
once the encoding fault was out of the way, BricsCAD V26's own shipped
=vle-extension.lsp= got further and then failed on ARITY.

The call is the vendor's, not ours —
=/Applications/BricsCAD V26.app/Contents/MacOS/vle-extension.lsp=,
offset 4452:

#+begin_example
(vl-catch-all-apply 'vl-load-com)
(setq *hasAX* (and vlax-get-acad-object))
#+end_example

BricsCAD loads this file at startup, so BricsCAD necessarily accepts a
one-argument =vl-catch-all-apply=.

* Problem

clautolisp requires both arguments in every dialect:

#+begin_example
$ clautolisp --no-init --dialect bricscad -x '(progn (setq *AUTOLISP-FILE-ENCODING* "mac-roman")
    (load "/Applications/BricsCAD V26.app/Contents/MacOS/vle-extension.lsp"))'
clautolisp: runtime error: BUILTIN-ERROR: AutoLISP builtin
  VL-CATCH-ALL-APPLY signaled an error: invalid number of arguments: 1
  in SUBR VL-CATCH-ALL-APPLY: (VL-LOAD-COM)
#+end_example

So no ALPM system that references the installed BricsCAD library can be
loaded under =--dialect bricscad=: the vendor's own file is rejected by
the dialect that claims to emulate its engine.

The builtin is =BUILTIN-VL-CATCH-ALL-APPLY=
(=autolisp-builtins-core/source/api.lisp=, =REQUIRE-PROPER-LIST= on the
second argument at line 669) and is registered as a fixed-arity SUBR.

* Proposal

Make the argument list optional — =(vl-catch-all-apply FUNCTION
[ARG-LIST])=, defaulting to =nil=, i.e. call FUNCTION with no arguments
— and decide the dialect scope. Two questions to settle before coding,
one of which needs a probe:

1. *Which dialects?* =bricscad-v26= is established by the vendor file
   above. Whether AutoCAD accepts the one-argument form is NOT known:
   nothing here has probed it. Do not guess — add it to the probe suite
   (=probes/sources/=) and let the answer decide between "bricscad +
   lax + clautolisp" and "every dialect".
2. *Does =--strict= keep rejecting it?* Consistent with the treatment
   of other vendor-specific tolerances, =--strict= should stay strict
   and emit the usual divergence diagnostic rather than silently
   accepting a form only one engine takes.

* Acceptance criteria

1. [X] =(vl-catch-all-apply 'f)= behaves as =(vl-catch-all-apply 'f nil)=
   in whichever dialects the probe establishes.
2. [X] A probe records the AutoCAD answer, so the dialect scope rests on
   a measurement rather than an assumption. *ANSWERED 2026-08-17, run
   =20260817T070448Z= on a real accoreconsole (AutoCAD 2022, Windows):*

   | case                                     | AutoCAD 2022                    |
   |------------------------------------------+---------------------------------|
   | control, =(vl-catch-all-apply f nil)=    | ok, 42                          |
   | argument list omitted                    | *ok, 42*                        |
   | omitted, function requires an argument   | error, "nombre d'arguments insuffisants" |

   So AutoCAD ACCEPTS it, and treats the missing list as EMPTY -- the
   same as BricsCAD and as clautolisp. The third case is the
   discriminating one; the French wording is the engine's own, which is
   also what proves the run came from AutoCAD and not from our baseline.
3. [X] Regression test pinned on the ARITY, not on that vendor file.
4. [X] Spec entry updated with the divergence.

* Resolution — 1.8.45

Both questions in the Proposal are settled, and the reasoning is here so
they are not re-litigated:

*Which dialects accept it silently?* SETTLED BY THE PROBE, and the answer
overturned the shipped design. Both engines accept it, so there is no
vendor divergence at all: =autocad=, =bricscad=, =clautolisp= and =lax=
are silent. Only =strict= warns, and for a different reason than the one
first implemented -- see below.

The first implementation had it backwards, warning under =--autocad= on
the strength of Autodesk's documentation. Documentation is not evidence
about an engine. That is exactly why the probe was written BEFORE the
dialect scope was decided, and it is the one part of this issue's
handling that worked as intended.

*Does --strict keep rejecting it?* It never rejects -- it accepts and
WARNS, because a dialect problem is flagged here, never raised, and
refusing would mean a vendor file cannot be loaded even to read it.

But the REASON for that warning changed after the measurement (pjb,
2026-08-17: "il y aura donc un nouveau warning dialect sur cette
forme"). It is no longer a =:vendor-divergence= -- the engines agree --
but a =:portability= hazard of a different kind: the tolerance is
UNDOCUMENTED by both vendors, and =--strict= is precisely the dialect
that asks for the documented subset. Two engines accepting a form today
is not a promise about tomorrow.

Registered as a =:vendor-divergence= warning, so it carries its own
annex entry and the consistency check fails if the tag is ever emitted
without one.

*Found while implementing*: the first version of the dialect predicate
tested the dialect NAME against =:bricscad=, and warned under
=--dialect bricscad= -- because that resolves to =:bricscad-v26=. Dialect
names are versioned; the PRODUCT is what a family test must use. Caught
by running it, not by reading it.

*Not done here*: criterion 3's original wording asked for a load of the
real =vle-extension.lsp=. That file is not redistributable and absent
from every non-macOS host, so a test using it would pass by being
skipped exactly where it matters. The regression pins the arity instead,
which is the thing that broke.

Tests: =clautolisp/autolisp-builtins-core/tests/builtin-tests.lisp=
(=VL-CATCH-ALL-APPLY-ACCEPTS-AN-OMITTED-ARGUMENT-LIST=). Spec 0.9.6 --
which also corrects a pre-existing drift: the header said =#+VERSION:
0.9.5= while the prose still announced 0.9.2.
